        "scanners": {
          "vt": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "analysis": "Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: team-dispatch\nVersion: 1.0.6\n\nThe skill bundle implements a complex multi-agent orchestration system that performs high-privilege operations and establishes background persistence. Key indicators include instructions in SKILL.md for the AI agent to autonomously execute a shell script (setup.sh) and maintain an event-driven scheduling loop without user intervention. The setup.sh script modifies the global openclaw.json configuration to grant broad sub-agent permissions (allowAgents: [\"*\"]), while watch-install.sh installs background tasks via launchd, systemd, or cron. While these behaviors are aligned with the stated purpose of workflow orchestration, the combination of autonomous execution instructions, global configuration tampering, and persistence mechanisms represents a significant security risk.",
            "source": "palm",
            "checkedAt": 1773035369337
          },
          "skillspector": null,
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The skill's files and instructions match a multi-agent orchestration tool, but its runtime instructions ask the agent to auto-run installation scripts that write system/user configs, restart the Gateway, and install persistent watchers — actions that are powerful and deserve manual review before trusting.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The name/description (multi-agent orchestration) aligns with what the skill does: it installs agent workspaces, templates, a DAG/task tracker, and a watcher. Creating agent workspaces, copying templates, and persisting task JSON files are coherent with the stated purpose. However, some requested changes (writing to openclaw.json, setting main.subagents.allowAgents=[\"*\"], restarting the Gateway, installing scheduled watchers) are higher privilege operations than a simple skill and should be explicitly justified and consented to by the user.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "SKILL.md instructs the agent to derive SKILL_DIR, then run scripts/setup.sh automatically (on first install and after updates). setup.sh will create symlinks, create/copy files into ~/.openclaw (agents, configs, workspace), write/modify openclaw.json (agents.list and allowAgents), create sessions directories, and restart the Gateway. It also documents installing OS scheduler artifacts (launchd plist or Windows scheduled task) to run watch.sh. These instructions go beyond harmless local guidance: they perform system configuration and persistent background task installation and they grant broad subagent permissions. The SKILL.md also demands the agent “must first execute” this detection/installation flow, which gives the skill broad discretion unless the user intervenes.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "There is no external install spec, but the package includes many local install/management scripts (setup.sh, watch.sh, watch-install.sh, watch.py, uninstall.sh, etc.) that are intended to be executed. No remote downloads are obvious from the manifest excerpts (no external URLs in SKILL.md), which reduces supply-chain risk, but running included scripts still writes files, creates symlinks, and may invoke system services (restart Gateway, install launchd/cron tasks). The presence of many executable scripts increases the attack surface and deserves manual inspection.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
